🚀 Day 23 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Today, I solved LeetCode 90 – Subsets II.

After solving Subsets I, today I took the next step and handled the challenge of duplicate elements. This problem helped me understand how a small modification in backtracking can prevent duplicate subsets from appearing in the result.

💡 Approach: Sorting + Recursion + Backtracking

The core idea remains the include/exclude pattern:

✅ Include the current element and continue recursion.
❌ Exclude it and move to the next valid position.
🔄 Before the exclude call, I skip consecutive duplicate elements so that the same subset isn't generated multiple times.

I first sorted the array, which makes duplicate elements adjacent. Then, after excluding an element, I move the index forward while the next elements are duplicates.

📚 What I Learned:

✅ Handling duplicates in backtracking
✅ Why sorting is useful before solving duplicate-based problems
✅ Using push_back() and pop_back() to maintain the current state
✅ The importance of making the right choice while moving to the next recursion level
✅ How to modify the classic include/exclude pattern to avoid duplicate results

⏱️ Time Complexity: O(n × 2ⁿ)
💾 Space Complexity: O(n) auxiliary recursion space (excluding the output)

